  "activity": {
    "id": "vsepr_exploration_activity",
    "name": "VSEPR Theory and Molecular Geometry Exploration",
    "description": "Students explore VSEPR theory by starting with Lewis structures, counting electron domains (steric number), and predicting electron-domain geometries. They then observe how lone pairs modify molecular geometry and compare predictions to real molecular structures (H2O, NH3, CH4, PF5, SF6, XeF4), learning why lone pairs occupy more angular space and cause bond-angle deviations from idealized values.",
    "workplace": "vsepr_chemistry_lab",
    "start": "step_1_lewis_to_steric",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_1_lewis_to_steric",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "From Lewis Structures to Steric Number",
          "description": "Begin by examining Lewis structures. The steric number is the total count of electron domains around a central atom: bonding pairs (each bond counts as one domain, whether single, double, or triple) plus lone pairs. For example, H2O has 2 bonding pairs and 2 lone pairs, giving a steric number of 4. Examine the Lewis structure image and identify the electron domains around the central oxygen atom."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "lewis_structures_station",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lewis_structure_image",
              "poi": "lewis_diagram_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "intro_audio_vsepr"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "steric_number_label",
              "poi": "steric_label_poi"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_1_complete"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2_electron_geometry",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Electron-Domain Geometry: Five Fundamental Shapes",
          "description": "The electron-domain geometry depends solely on the steric number. Five steric numbers produce five electron-domain geometries: steric number 2 \u2192 linear, 3 \u2192 trigonal planar, 4 \u2192 tetrahedral, 5 \u2192 trigonal bipyramidal, and 6 \u2192 octahedral. These geometries minimize electron-pair repulsion. Watch the animations showing how electron domains arrange in three-dimensional space to achieve maximum separation and minimum repulsion."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_sphere",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "electron_geometry_animation",
              "poi": "domain_animation_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_sphere",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "five_geometries_animation",
              "poi": "domain_animation_poi"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_sphere",
              "augmentation": "electron_geometry_animation"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_2_complete"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3_lone_pair_effects",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "How Lone Pairs Modify Molecular Geometry",
          "description": "Electron-domain geometry describes the 3D arrangement of all domains (bonding and lone pairs). Molecular geometry describes only the positions of atoms. When one or more domains are lone pairs, the molecular geometry differs from the electron-domain geometry. Lone pairs occupy more angular space than bonding pairs because they are attracted only to one nucleus. This causes the remaining bonding pairs to compress, lowering bond angles below idealized values. For example, water has a tetrahedral electron-domain geometry (steric number 4) but a bent molecular geometry because 2 domains are lone pairs."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "electron_domain_sphere",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lone_pair_effect_animation",
              "poi": "domain_animation_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecular_structure_models",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "water_structure_model",
              "poi": "water_poi"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_3_complete"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_4_bond_angles",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Bond-Angle Deviations from Ideal Values",
          "description": "Idealized electron-domain geometries predict specific bond angles: linear (180\u00b0), trigonal planar (120\u00b0), tetrahedral (109.5\u00b0), trigonal bipyramidal (90\u00b0 and 120\u00b0), and octahedral (90\u00b0). Lone pairs compress these angles by occupying more space. Water's H-O-H angle is 104.5\u00b0 rather than 109.5\u00b0 due to its two lone pairs on oxygen. Ammonia (NH3) has an H-N-H angle of 107\u00b0 rather than 109.5\u00b0. Study the comparison image showing measured bond angles versus predictions."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "geometry_prediction_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "bond_angle_comparison_image",
              "poi": "comparison_visual_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecular_structure_models",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ammonia_structure_model",
              "poi": "ammonia_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecular_structure_models",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "methane_structure_model",
              "poi": "methane_poi"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_4_complete"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_5_real_molecules",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Applying VSEPR to Real Molecules",
          "description": "Apply VSEPR theory to predict the structures of six key molecules and compare to experimental observations. Methane (CH4) has steric number 4, all bonding pairs \u2192 tetrahedral geometry and 109.5\u00b0 bond angles. Water (H2O) has steric number 4 with 2 lone pairs \u2192 bent geometry and 104.5\u00b0 angles. Ammonia (NH3) has steric number 4 with 1 lone pair \u2192 trigonal pyramidal geometry and 107\u00b0 angles. Phosphorus pentafluoride (PF5) has steric number 5 \u2192 trigonal bipyramidal geometry. Sulfur hexafluoride (SF6) has steric number 6 \u2192 octahedral geometry. Xenon tetrafluoride (XeF4) has steric number 6 with 2 lone pairs \u2192 square planar geometry. Observe how predictions match experimental structures and understand the geometric consequences of lone pairs."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "molecular_structure_models",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pf5_structure_model",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecular_structure_models",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "sf6_structure_model",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecular_structure_models",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "xef4_structure_model",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "geometry_prediction_zone",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "summary_label",
              "poi": "comparison_visual_poi"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_5_complete"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
